---
import PageLayout from 'blume/components/layout/PageLayout.astro';
import data from 'blume:data';

const pageDescription = 'Typed RPC for React. One Result and one wire-safe error union per procedure.';

---

<PageLayout
  site={{ title: data.config.title, description: data.config.description }}
  logo={data.config.logo}
  favicon={data.config.favicon}
  appleIcon={data.config.appleIcon}
  banner={data.config.banner}
  analytics={data.config.analytics}
  navigation={data.navigation}
  themeMode={data.config.theme.mode}
  fontCssVars={data.fontCssVars}
  searchEnabled={data.config.search.enabled}
  siteUrl={data.config.site}
  ogEnabled={data.config.og.enabled}
  ogImage="/og-home.png"
  page={{
    title: 'result-rpc — typed RPC for React',
    description: pageDescription,
    route: '/',
  }}
>
  <header class="nav shell">
    <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="result-rpc home">
      <img src="/result-rpc-lockup-blue.svg" alt="result-rpc" width="206" height="41" />
    </a>
    <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
      <a href="/start/introduction/">Docs</a>
      <a href="/concepts/shells/">Shells</a>
      <a href="/concepts/entities/">Entities</a>
      <a href="/guides/migrating-from-trpc/">From tRPC</a>
      <a class="github" href="https://github.com/jokull/result-rpc">GitHub <span>↗</span></a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <div class="home-content">
    <section class="hero shell">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <div class="kicker"><span></span> A protocol for robust React apps</div>
        <h1>Errors are values.<br /><em>Values have owners.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">There's a day when offline behavior, 5xx, session expiry and stale deploys stop being polish and become the work. This is the layer that already has owners for them.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button primary" href="/start/quickstart/">Read the quickstart <span>→</span></a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="/start/introduction/">Read the introduction</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-visual">
        <figure class="hero-code" aria-label="The same call, unnarrowed and under the shells">
          <pre><code><span class="c">// the honest base: the complete union</span>
<span class="k">const</span> query = useResultQuery(client.doc.byId, &#123; id &#125;)
<span class="c">// DocNotFound | Unauthorized | ServerInternal | Offline |
// NetworkFailure | Timeout | HttpFailure |
// ProtocolViolation | DecodeFailure | Stale</span>

<span class="c">// with shells mounted: each one subtracted what it owns</span>
<span class="k">const</span> query = AuthShell.useQuery(client.doc.byId, &#123; id &#125;)
<span class="c">// DocNotFound — and a missing case fails to compile</span></code></pre>
          <figcaption>Nothing was hidden. Each tag left the union because a named owner took responsibility for it.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pillars shell" aria-labelledby="pillars-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-label">Four ideas, one library</p>
        <h2 id="pillars-title">What you get that tRPC can't give you.</h2>
      </div>

      <article class="pillar">
        <div class="pillar-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">01 · Stacked error contexts</p>
          <h3>Errors have addresses.</h3>
          <p>Every layer you compose <em>adds</em> to a procedure's error union — auth adds <code>Unauthorized</code>, the framework adds <code>Offline</code>, <code>Timeout</code>, <code>Stale</code>. On the client, shells <em>subtract</em> what they own at the junction in the tree that should own it: a banner claims offline, an auth boundary claims session expiry, a dialog owns the conflict.</p>
          <p>By the time a leaf reads the union it holds only the domain errors that component can render — and a missing case fails to compile. Not a global <code>onError</code>, not a handler at every call site. Handled <strong>positionally</strong>, once.</p>
          <a class="pillar-link" href="/concepts/shells/">How shells claim failures <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
        <figure class="pillar-code">
          <pre><code><span class="c">// SERVER — every layer stacks onto the union</span>
<span class="k">const</span> feed = app.procedure()
  .use(authed)              <span class="c">// + Unauthorized</span>
  .errors(&#123; RateLimited &#125;)   <span class="c">// + RateLimited</span>
  .output(Post.pick(<span class="s">"id"</span>, <span class="s">"body"</span>)).query(handler)

<span class="c">// CLIENT — shells peel it back, where it belongs</span>
&lt;OfflineBanner&gt;      <span class="c">// claims Offline, Timeout</span>
  &lt;SessionShell&gt;     <span class="c">// claims Unauthorized → redirect</span>
    &lt;Feed /&gt;         <span class="c">// sees RateLimited | PostNotFound. done.</span></code></pre>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="pillar">
        <div class="pillar-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">02 · The wire contract</p>
          <h3>The wire is not JSON.</h3>
          <p><code>Date</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>Set</code>, <code>BigInt</code>, cycles — they cross intact and your return types stay clean, because the codec carries the complexity, not you. Declare a <em>model</em> and the client tracks it by identity. The same contract language reaches Standard Schema for forms and your Drizzle tables for entities — one source of truth.</p>
          <p>GraphQL makes you model the whole graph to get identity. Because <em>you</em> own the wire, you breathe entities, scalars, and one-off aggregates into whatever shape a screen needs — no schema ceremony, no selection sets — and identity still holds on exactly what you return.</p>
          <a class="pillar-link" href="/concepts/wire/">Rich types on the wire <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
        <figure class="pillar-code">
          <pre><code><span class="k">const</span> search = app.procedure()
  .output(wire.object(&#123;
    hotel:  Hotel.pick(<span class="s">"id"</span>, <span class="s">"name"</span>), <span class="c">// entity — patches by id</span>
    openAt: wire.date,                <span class="c">// a real Date, not a string</span>
    from:   wire.number,              <span class="c">// a one-off aggregate</span>
  &#125;))
  .query(handler)

<span class="c">// client receives: &#123; hotel, openAt: Date, from &#125;</span>
<span class="c">// clean types. no codegen. no selection set.</span></code></pre>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="pillar">
        <div class="pillar-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">03 · The client boundary</p>
          <h3>We ship a client, not a type.</h3>
          <p>tRPC ships its router as a <em>type</em>, erased at build. result-rpc ships a real runtime built from your contract — which is why codecs run on the wire, why the entity cache exists at all, and why rich types survive the trip.</p>
          <p>It costs one discipline: import the <em>contract</em>, never the <em>router</em>. And that discipline is a <strong>security feature</strong> — a model never reaches an output un-scoped, and your server's type graph never leaks into a consumer's <code>tsc</code>. A real client in your bundle, and nothing behind it.</p>
          <a class="pillar-link" href="/concepts/client-boundary/">The client boundary rule <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
        <figure class="pillar-code">
          <pre><code><span class="c">// ✅ build the client from the CONTRACT —</span>
<span class="c">//    a runtime of codecs, and nothing else</span>
<span class="k">import</span> &#123; appContract &#125; <span class="k">from</span> <span class="s">"./contract"</span>
<span class="k">export const</span> client = createClient(&#123;
  contract: appContract, transport,
&#125;)

<span class="c">// ☠️ importing the ROUTER instead ships your</span>
<span class="c">//    handlers, db driver, and secrets to the</span>
<span class="c">//    browser. bundlers won't save you.</span></code></pre>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="pillar">
        <div class="pillar-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">04 · Client freshness</p>
          <h3>An invalidation ladder, not a tapestry.</h3>
          <p>Keeping the UI current shouldn't be a hand-maintained web of optimistic updates and invalidations on the client. Entities are <em>addressable</em> — a keyed row — so when you author the mutation, the client already knows what changed. Return the entity and every cached view patches by id, for free, zero refetches.</p>
          <p>Above that: <code>.affects()</code> declares which lists gain or lose the row, and the server can <code>touch()</code> cascades the output can't name. Underneath it all is <strong>TanStack Query core</strong> — stale timers and refetch logic are the floor. What we don't do is peer-to-peer sync; this is one client, kept incrementally in shape.</p>
          <a class="pillar-link" href="/concepts/entities/">The entity cache <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
        <figure class="pillar-code">
          <pre><code><span class="c">// FREE — return the entity, every view patches by id</span>
rename: app.procedure()
  .output(User.pick(<span class="s">"id"</span>, <span class="s">"name"</span>)).mutation(handler)

<span class="c">// MEMBERSHIP — which lists change, declared once</span>
comment: app.procedure().affects(commentsFor).mutation(handler)

<span class="c">// CASCADES the output can't name — the server touches them</span>
archive: app.procedure().mutation((&#123; touch &#125;) =&gt; &#123;
  touch(Task, id); <span class="c">/* … */</span>
&#125;)</code></pre>
        </figure>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="faq shell" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-label">Questions you're about to ask</p>
        <h2 id="faq-title">Straight answers.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary><span>Does it work with React Server Components?</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Yes. There's a <code>dehydrate</code>/<code>hydrate</code> bridge and a nestable <code>&lt;ResultRpcHydrationBoundary&gt;</code>: prefetch on the server, and the client renders on first paint with zero requests. Hydrated entities are indexed too, so a client mutation patches a server-rendered row in place. There are working examples for Waku, Next.js App Router, and TanStack Start.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary><span>Why <em>Result</em>? Why not just throw?</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Every JavaScript codebase that wants safety eventually reaches for errors-as-values — it's how safe languages work, and it bridges cleanly into TypeScript. There are excellent libraries for it: better-result, neverthrow, Effect. We vendored a slimmed-down port of the better-result API because it's genuinely nice to use.</p>
            <p>But we needed one thing they don't give you: <strong>wire safety</strong>. A tagged error has to survive a codec and cross the network. The same <code>Result&lt;T, E&gt;</code> the server returns is the one your component reads.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary><span>How is this different from returning <code>Result</code> from a tRPC procedure?</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>tRPC leaves you with two error layers: domain errors inside the <code>data</code> envelope, and transport/framework errors on <code>query.error</code>. Every component has to reconcile both. result-rpc collapses them — domain, transport, protocol, and staleness — into a single closed union per operation. One channel, one <code>switch</code>, exhaustive or it won't compile.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary><span>Is it inspired by GraphQL?</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>By its error ergonomics, yes. GraphQL — and urql's Graphcache in particular — popularized modeling a failure as a value in a union you can exhaustively handle, plus a normalized cache keyed by identity. We took both ideas. We left the rigidity: no mandatory schema-of-the-whole-graph, no selection sets, no resolver layer. Because you own the wire contract, you compose entities and scalars into whatever a screen needs.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary><span>Do I have to use Drizzle?</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>No. Models are plain wire codecs you can hand-write. Drizzle is one optional subpath that <em>derives</em> them from your tables so a column allowlist is the whole entity contract — but nothing requires it.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary><span>Which server framework does it need?</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>None in particular. The handler is a standard <code>Request → Response</code> function, so it mounts on Hono, Next.js route handlers, Bun, Deno, Cloudflare Workers — anything that speaks the Web platform.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary><span>Is it realtime? Does it sync across clients?</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Subscriptions push server→client over SSE, and the entity cache patches from those events. But there's no peer-to-peer sync and no authoritative stream fanned out to every client — that's a different product (look at Rocicorp's Zero). This is one client, kept incrementally in shape on TanStack Query core.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary><span>Can I keep my Zod / Valibot schemas? And file uploads?</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Validators plug in through Standard Schema, for inputs and for forms. Binaries stay out of band on purpose: upload to object storage (a presigned <code>PUT</code>) and put a bucket reference on the contract — which keeps the wire small and the CSRF surface uniform.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary><span>Is it production-ready?</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>It's <code>0.1.0</code> — pre-1.0, and the API surface can still move before it settles. What's there is heavily tested (counter-pinned request assertions, a seeded coherence oracle, the same app built on three frameworks), but you should treat a pre-1.0 pin as pre-1.0.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta">
      <div class="shell cta-inner">
        <div>
          <p class="section-label">Coming from tRPC?</p>
          <h2>Migrate per router.<br />Start with the auth layer.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="cta-actions">
          <a class="button primary light" href="/guides/migrating-from-trpc/">Read the migration guide <span>→</span></a>
          <a href="/reference/examples/">Browse the examples ladder</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <footer class="shell" slot="footer">
    <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="result-rpc home"><img src="/result-rpc-lockup-blue.svg" alt="result-rpc" width="174" height="35" /></a>
    <p>One Result and one wire-safe error union per procedure.</p>
    <div><a href="/reference/sharp-edges/">Sharp edges</a><a href="https://github.com/jokull/result-rpc">GitHub</a></div>
  </footer>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  :global(*) { box-sizing: border-box; }
  :global(html) { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
  :global(body) { margin: 0; overflow-x: clip; color: #111111; background: #f6f1e7; font-family: var(--blume-font-body); }
  :global([data-blume-header]) { display: none; }
  :global(a) { color: inherit; }
  .shell { width: min(1200px, calc(100% - 48px)); margin-inline: auto; }
  .nav { height: 92px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; border-bottom: 2px solid #2e5090; }
  .brand { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; text-decoration: none; }
  .brand img { display: block; width: 224px; height: auto; }
  .nav nav { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 30px; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 500; }
  .nav nav a { text-decoration: none; color: #3f4644; }
  .nav nav a:hover { color: #2e5090; }
  .nav nav .github { padding-left: 29px; border-left: 1px solid rgba(17,17,17,.2); color: #111111; font-weight: 600; }
  .nav nav .github span { color: #2e5090; }
  .hero { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.08fr .92fr; gap: 58px; align-items: center; min-height: 650px; padding-block: 76px 88px; }
  .kicker, .section-label, .eyebrow { color: #2e5090; font: 600 11px/1.3 var(--blume-font-mono); letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .kicker { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px; }
  .kicker span { width: 34px; height: 3px; background: #e7d28a; }
  h1 { margin: 25px 0; color: #111111; font: 700 clamp(55px, 5.6vw, 82px)/.96 var(--blume-font-display); letter-spacing: -.058em; }
  h1 em { color: #2e5090; font-style: normal; font-weight: 500; }
  .lede { max-width: 610px; margin: 0; color: #3f4846; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.68; }
  .actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 14px; margin-top: 34px; }
  .button { min-height: 50px; display: inline-flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; gap: 16px; padding: 0 21px; border: 1px solid rgba(17,17,17,.24); border-radius: 5px; text-decoration: none; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; }
  .button.primary { color: #fffdf7; background: #2e5090; border-color: #2e5090; }
  .button.primary:hover { color: #fffdf7; background: #213e76; border-color: #213e76; }
  .button.secondary { color: #111111; background: #fffdf7; }
  .button.secondary:hover { color: #2e5090; border-color: #2e5090; }
  .hero-visual { min-width: 0; }
  .hero-code { width: 100%; margin: 0; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid #111111; border-radius: 13px; color: #f6f1e7; background: #111111; box-shadow: -13px 16px 0 #e7d28a; transform: rotate(1deg); }
  .hero-code pre { margin: 0; padding: 28px; overflow-x: auto; font: 400 13px/1.72 var(--blume-font-mono); }
  .hero-code .c { color: #a8c5d9; }
  .hero-code .k { color: #e7d28a; }
  .hero-code figcaption { padding: 15px 28px; border-top: 1px solid #303030; color: #c1d6e3; font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.55; }
  .section-heading { margin-bottom: 44px; }
  .section-heading h2, .cta h2 { margin: 12px 0 0; font: 700 clamp(34px, 3.8vw, 50px)/1.02 var(--blume-font-display); letter-spacing: -.05em; }

  /* --- Four pillars, zigzag rhythm --------------------------------------- */
  .pillars { padding-block: 40px 104px; }
  .pillar { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 56px; align-items: center; padding-block: 54px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(17,17,17,.14); }
  .pillar:first-of-type { border-top: 0; }
  /* zigzag: on even rows the code takes column 1, copy column 2 */
  .pillar:nth-child(even) .pillar-copy { order: 2; }
  .pillar:nth-child(even) .pillar-code { order: 1; }
  .pillar-copy h3 { margin: 14px 0 18px; color: #111111; font: 700 clamp(28px, 3vw, 38px)/1.04 var(--blume-font-display); letter-spacing: -.04em; }
  .pillar-copy p { margin: 0 0 16px; color: #3f4846; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.66; }
  .pillar-copy p em { color: #2e5090; font-style: normal; font-weight: 600; }
  .pillar-copy p strong { color: #111111; font-weight: 700; }
  .pillar-copy code { padding: .08em .35em; border-radius: 4px; background: rgba(46,80,144,.1); color: #213e76; font: 500 .86em/1 var(--blume-font-mono); }
  .pillar-link { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 9px; margin-top: 4px; color: #2e5090; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; }
  .pillar-link span { transition: transform .15s ease; }
  .pillar-link:hover span { transform: translateX(4px); }
  .pillar-code { margin: 0; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid #111111; border-radius: 12px; background: #111111; box-shadow: -11px 14px 0 #e7d28a; }
  .pillar:nth-child(even) .pillar-code { box-shadow: 11px 14px 0 #a8c5d9; }
  .pillar-code pre { margin: 0; padding: 26px; overflow-x: auto; font: 400 12.5px/1.7 var(--blume-font-mono); color: #f6f1e7; }
  .pillar-code .c { color: #a8c5d9; }
  .pillar-code .k { color: #e7d28a; }
  .pillar-code .s { color: #cdb96a; }
  /* --- FAQ ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .faq { padding-block: 20px 104px; }
  .faq-list { display: grid; gap: 0; border-top: 1px solid rgba(17,17,17,.16); }
  .faq-item { border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(17,17,17,.16); }
  .faq-item summary { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 24px; padding: 22px 4px; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; color: #111111; font: 600 clamp(17px, 1.7vw, 20px)/1.35 var(--blume-font-display); letter-spacing: -.02em; }
  .faq-item summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .faq-item summary > span { display: block; }
  .faq-item summary::after { flex: none; width: 13px; height: 13px; border-right: 2px solid #2e5090; border-bottom: 2px solid #2e5090; transform: rotate(45deg) translate(-3px, -3px); transition: transform .18s ease; content: ""; }
  .faq-item[open] summary::after { transform: rotate(225deg) translate(-3px, -3px); }
  .faq-item summary:hover { color: #2e5090; }
  .faq-item summary em { color: #2e5090; font-style: normal; }
  .faq-item summary code { padding: .08em .34em; border-radius: 4px; background: rgba(46,80,144,.1); color: #213e76; font: 500 .82em/1 var(--blume-font-mono); }
  .faq-body { padding: 0 4px 26px; max-width: 78ch; }
  .faq-body p { margin: 0 0 13px; color: #3f4846; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.68; }
  .faq-body p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .faq-body strong { color: #111111; font-weight: 700; }
  .faq-body em { color: #2e5090; font-style: normal; font-weight: 600; }
  .faq-body code { padding: .08em .35em; border-radius: 4px; background: rgba(46,80,144,.1); color: #213e76; font: 500 .86em/1 var(--blume-font-mono); }
  .cta { position: relative; overflow: hidden; color: #fffdf7; background: #2e5090; }
  .cta::after { position: absolute; inset: -65% -8% auto 55%; width: 560px; aspect-ratio: 1; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.18); border-radius: 50%; box-shadow: 0 0 0 48px rgba(255,255,255,.045), 0 0 0 96px rgba(255,255,255,.035); content: ""; }
  .cta-inner { position: relative; z-index: 1; padding-block: 86px; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 40px; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }
  .cta .section-label { color: #e7d28a; }
  .cta-actions { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 26px; }
  .cta-actions > a:last-child { color: #dce8f0; font-size: 14px; }
  .button.primary.light { color: #21304b; background: #fffdf7; border-color: #fffdf7; }
  .button.primary.light:hover { color: #111111; background: #e7d28a; border-color: #e7d28a; }
  footer.shell { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 20px; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; padding-block: 42px; color: #4a5351; font-size: 13px; }
  footer .brand img { width: 174px; }
  footer div { display: flex; gap: 22px; }
  footer a { text-decoration: none; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hero { grid-template-columns: 1fr; min-height: 0; gap: 54px; padding-block: 55px 76px; }
    .hero-code { max-width: 680px; }
    /* stack pillars: copy first, code after, on every row */
    .pillar { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 32px; padding-block: 44px; }
    .pillar:nth-child(even) .pillar-copy,
    .pillar:nth-child(even) .pillar-code { order: 0; }
    .pillar:nth-child(even) .pillar-code { box-shadow: -11px 14px 0 #e7d28a; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 680px) {
    .shell { width: min(100% - 30px, 1200px); }
    .nav { height: 78px; }
